<article class="env-lemma" id="02K0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02K0">Lemma <span data-tag="02K0">29.31.13</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $R \to A = R[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ c)$ be a relative global complete intersection. Set $S = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R)$ and $X = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$. Consider the morphism $f : X \to S$ associated to the ring map $R \to A$. The function $x \mapsto \dim _ x(X_{f(x)})$ is constant with value $n - c$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/00SP" data-tag="00SP">10.136.5</a> $R \to A$ being a relative global complete intersection means all nonzero fibre rings have dimension $n - c$. Thus for a prime $\mathfrak p$ of $R$ the fibre ring $\kappa (\mathfrak p)[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(\overline{f}_1, \ldots , \overline{f}_ c)$ is either zero or a global complete intersection ring of dimension $n - c$. By the discussion following Algebra, Definition <a href="/tag/00S9" data-tag="00S9">10.135.1</a> this implies it is equidimensional of dimension $n - c$. Whence the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>